== A Master’s Care for His Servants’ Souls and Bodies

=== §. 21. #emph[Of masters seeking the salvation of their servants.]

The #emph[justice] required of masters concerns the #emph[soul, body,] and #emph[livelihood] of their servants.

With respect to the good of their souls, masters must seek the spiritual #footnote[I. #emph[Iusti patres secundum haec temporalia bona, filiorum sortem à seruorum conditione distinguebant: ad Deum autem colendum, in quo aeterna bona speranda sunt, omnibus domus suae membris pari dilectione consulebant. Aug. de Ciu. Dei l.] 19. #emph[c.] 15.] edification of their servants. When Zacchaeus first believed, Christ said, “Salvation has come to this house” (Luke 19:9). Why to #emph[this house,] rather than to #emph[this person,] except because he knew that Zacchaeus would do the duty of a good master and seek the salvation of his household? In this, masters must show an impartial regard to all in their house, as the holy fathers did: though they made a difference between the place of children and the condition of servants in matters of temporal goods, yet in serving God, in which eternal happiness is looked for, they provided with equal regard for all the members of their house. The proofs given for servants’ obedience to their masters’ care in this point show that this duty belongs to masters. See Treatise 7, §. 15.

The regard which masters owe to God, to themselves, to their servants, and to the church and commonwealth where they live, requires as much.

1\. God has commanded as much (Deut 6:7) as this charge implies: “You shall talk of My laws when you sit at home.” And God has shown his approval of it by commending Abraham for commanding his children and #emph[household] to keep the way of the Lord, Gen 18:19.

2\. Masters themselves reap great benefit from a faithful discharge #footnote[#emph[De salute eorum qui in domo tua sunt sollicitus, ac peruigil existas, quia pro omnibus tibi subiectis rationem Domino reddes. Aug. de salut. docum. cap.] 29.] of this duty, and that both by keeping a good conscience toward God (who requires this at their hands, since he has made them prophets and priests in their house, as well as kings, and will require an account from them for all who are under their government), and also by bringing their servants to do more faithful service to them. For there is no such means to stir up servants to do every good duty as the fear of God planted in their hearts. The servant who finds true grace either first worked, or further increased, in him by his master’s means will think himself so indebted to such a master that he will never be able to make any sufficient recompense; and therefore he will strive to do what good service he can by way of thankfulness. He will not only be faithful and diligent in his business, but he will call on God to prosper his service for his master’s good, and to repay the kindness his master has done him.

3\. No earthly thing that a master can do for his servant (be it a portion of money, preferment to any place of profit or reputation, or skill in a good trade and calling) can compare with the edifying of a servant in grace.

4\. Servants well instructed in piety are the most likely to turn out most profitable, not only to the family, but also to the church and commonwealth where they live.

So that masters may edify their servants the better:

1\. They must instruct them daily in the principles of religion #footnote[Directions for edifying servants.] and all the duties of piety. Wonderful is the profit that will come from a daily and constant use of religious exercises: though only a little time is spent at once, yet much knowledge will be gained by using them often. This duty is all the more necessary because public ministers cannot take such particular notice of every servant in their parishes as masters can in their families.

2\. Masters must make their servants go to the public ministry of the word and the worship of God, to be further built up by it and confirmed in their faith. Masters under the law were #footnote[Exod 12:44.] commanded to let their servants eat the Passover, which was a solemn sacrament. The law which requires all males to appear #footnote[Deut 16:16.] before the Lord on the solemn feast days implies that servants also should go. This duty must especially be performed on the Lord’s day, for the charge given to masters in the fourth commandment for sanctifying that day is extended to servants in these words: “neither you, … nor your manservant or maidservant.”

3\. Masters, besides instructing servants at home and making them go to church, must take an account of their progress, both by the public and by the private means of edification. Otherwise they will not know how to order their way of instructing them: when to give them milk, and when solid food.

4\. To make the means more effectual and profitable, they must add prayer to instruction. Means without God’s blessing on the means are nothing. As they observe any grace worked in their servants, they must be thankful to God for it and pray for its increase.

=== §. 22. #emph[Of masters neglecting to edify their servants.]

Contrary to this is the mind and practice of most masters. They think that if they allow their servants sufficient food, lodging and clothing, or wages according to their agreement, they have done all they need to do; and accordingly they do no more. In this they show themselves no better than the heathen. For do not the heathen do the same?

#emph[Objection.] When the servant was first taken on, no more was agreed in the covenant.

#emph[Answer.] There are two covenants by which a master stands bound: one with God, the other with his servant. Though his covenant with his servant requires no more than some temporal benefits, yet God’s covenant requires spiritual edification. Many masters are so greedy for their servants’ work that they are unwilling to allow any time, morning or evening, for religious exercises. They think they will thrive by their servants’ labor, and do not think of God’s blessing, which makes rich. Some go so far in this that they keep their servants from the public worship of God, even on the Lord’s day. So it comes about that servants who came to a master ignorant and profane go away, after a long stay with him, just as they came. Many who themselves make some conscience of fearing God fail greatly in this. They do not care to teach their servants the fear of God, and so they deprive themselves of much blessing and pull God’s curse on their persons and houses.

=== §. 23. #emph[Of allowing servants sufficient food.]

Since servants do not have bodies of brass or steel, #footnote[II.] but of flesh and blood like all others, masters who have the benefit of their strength and of the ability of their bodies must take care to nourish and cherish them, and that both in #emph[health] and in #emph[sickness.]

For preserving servants’ health, regard must be had to their #emph[food, clothing, labor, rest.]

A due provision of food for servants is commended in Solomon’s #footnote[Sufficient food to be given to servants. Prov 31:15; 27:27.] good housewife, who is said “to provide food for her household”; and in the direction he gives to householders in these words: “Let the milk of your goats be enough for your food, for the food of your household.”

The food masters provide for their servants must be, for #emph[quality,] good and wholesome; for #emph[quantity,] sufficient to preserve health and increase strength; for #emph[time,] given in due season. It is noted that #emph[the hired servants] of the father of the #footnote[Luke 15:17.] prodigal son had #emph[bread] (by #emph[bread,] in the phrase of Scripture, is meant every kind of needful and wholesome food; and it must be taken in that sense there, because it is set against #emph[husks,] which are not very wholesome and are fitter for swine than for men); indeed, they had #emph[bread enough.] It is further noted of that good steward who was, as a master, #emph[ruler over the household,] that #footnote[Luke 12:42.] he gave the household #emph[their portion of food in due season.]

#emph[Question.] May servants be stinted in their food?

#emph[Answer.] As to #emph[excess,] they may and ought to be stinted, but not as to #emph[sufficiency.] It is not fitting that all servants should have as much as they can devour, for then many of them would do only a little work. But it is most fitting that everyone should have as much as is needful for strength, so that he may be the better able to do and endure his work. The Greek word translated #emph[portion] in the #footnote[Luke 12:42 σιτομέτριον.] place quoted before implies as much.

There is a double bond to tie masters to perform this duty: one with respect to themselves, the other with respect to their servants.

Masters themselves will have the profit and benefit of their servants’ health and strength, so for their own sakes it is needful to give them sufficient food. Men who want their work done well by their beasts, or want to be carried well to the end of their journey, will take care that their beasts are well fed.

But besides this, since the health and strength of servants is spent in their masters’ business, justice requires that their health and strength should be repaired and preserved by their masters.

=== §. 24. #emph[Of defect and excess in allowing servants food.]

There are two extremes contrary to this duty. One is that of those who are too stingy and (as we say) miserly in giving food to their servants. Sometimes this is in the quantity, when servants’ bellies are pinched too much, even so that their bodies are weakened for lack of food. The prodigal son was served like this.#footnote[Luke 15:16.] These are both unjust and harmful masters: unjust to their servants, harmful to their servants and to themselves too. Sometimes again such miserly masters offend in the quality of the food they give their servants: as when it is kept too long and has grown musty, moldy or otherwise unsavory; or when the worst kind of food is bought for cheapness’ sake, even such as is hardly fit for a man to eat. The greater the abundance of such stuff, the more loathsome it is.

Lastly, though I do not think it fitting to bind masters to set hours for their servants’ meals (Christ allows masters a greater #footnote[Luke 17:7, #emph[etc.]] liberty in the parable of a master’s treatment of his servant after he came in from his work), yet there may be a fault, as there is in many masters, in keeping their servants too long from food: not allowing them to break off their work for food’s sake, but making them wait for their supper until ten o’clock at night, when they stop work. Surely this cannot be good for the body’s health and strength.

Those masters fall into another extreme who #emph[bring up their] #footnote[Prov 29:21.] #emph[servants too delicately.] Solomon has expressly blamed such masters, and notes that this harm is likely to follow from it: “he will have him become his son in the end.” Such a servant will forget his place, scorn to be as a servant, and aspire to be as his master’s child, which is next to being his master’s equal.

=== §. 25. #emph[Of masters’ care about their servants’ clothing.]

A wise care for servants’ clothing is also commended in the example of Solomon’s good housewife: “She has no fear of the snow for her household,” that is, for the coldest season in winter, “for all her household are clothed in #footnote[Prov 31:21. שנים] double garments,” that is, with such clothing as is fit for cold weather.#footnote[The Hebrew word is often used for scarlet. But according to the root meaning of it, it signifies things doubled, which I take to be most fitting in this place, and therefore I have translated it so. The King James translators have noted as much in the margin.]

Fit and decent clothing is both a means of preserving health and also a matter of good report, tending to the reputation of a master. Yet the attitude of many is contrary to this: they do not care how tattered and ragged their servants’ clothing is, so much so that many servants have neither decent nor warm clothing.

This point especially concerns those masters who (as we say) keep their servants and provide everything for them, as in old times masters did for most servants, and as they now do for apprentices, whether male or female. If by agreement servants are to provide their own clothing, masters are not so strictly bound in this; yet they must take care that their servants fall neither into the one extreme of too shabby, slovenly or dirty clothing, nor into the other of too gaudy or too costly clothing. Masters, having authority over their servants, must keep them in order and within good bounds, and have regard to decency in this as in other things. For clothing is one of those outward signs by which the wisdom of masters and mistresses in governing their servants well is shown to the world. If therefore servants are dressed unsuitably for their place and means, all who see them will think that their masters and mistresses are of the same mind as the servants, or at least too lax and careless in their government.

=== §. 26. #emph[Of moderating servants’ labor.]

Though labor and pains belong to a servant’s place, yet he may be put to it so hard that the health and strength of his body is impaired by it. For the right ordering of this, therefore, a duty lies on masters, and that in two things especially.

1\. That they moderate well the labor they put their servants to, so that they may be able to bear it for the time and to hold out as long as their time of labor is appointed. It was a good reason which Saul gave to keep David from #footnote[1 Sam 17:33.] entering into combat with Goliath, because (as he thought) #emph[David was not able to fight against the Philistine, David being a youth, and Goliath a man of war.]

And indeed, if David’s faith had not exceeded the strength of his body, it would have been unfitting to put David to that task.

Those who are put to things beyond their ability are likely to fail and sink under the burden of them.

It is contrary to this to put servants to such hard tasks as impair their strength, endanger their limbs and risk their lives. Saul will rise up in judgment against such masters: for he, supposing that it was too hard a task for David to undertake combat with Goliath, was unwilling to put him to it.#footnote[1 Sam 17:33.] David failed greatly in this when he gave orders that Uriah should be set “at the front of the fiercest battle,”#footnote[2 Sam 11:15.] and yet that the commander with his forces should withdraw from him. Many servants, being put to services beyond their strength, either lose their lives by it or (as we say) are never their own men again.

It is also a fault of this kind to put servants to such toiling #footnote[#emph[Memineris seruos homines esse. Cato.] \[Remember that servants are men.\] Exod 1:13, 14.] work as is fitter for beasts than for men, or to oppress them with too much work, as the Egyptians oppressed the Israelites. Let God’s hearing the cry of those servants so oppressed,#footnote[Exod 3:7.] and his taking vengeance on their oppressors for it, make all masters beware of the like cruelty.

=== §. 27. #emph[Of providing servants with fit means for their work.]

2\. The other thing required of masters for the right ordering of their servants’ work is that they provide their servants with things needful and helpful for the work they are put to. Such a mind was commendable in Saul, who tried whether his #footnote[1 Sam 17:38.] armor might fit David when he was to go against Goliath. To list all the particulars would be an endless task: every separate trade and work has its own means of help belonging to it. This general direction may be enough to move masters to apply it to the particular works and services they put their servants to. Fit means are such a help that what may easily be done with them can hardly, if at all, be done without them.

It is contrary to this to deal with servants as the Egyptians did with the Israelites,#footnote[Exod 5:7.] demanding work from them and not providing them with the means to do it; by which they often demand impossibilities. Some will have much work done at night and not provide candlelight; and so in other particulars.

=== §. 28. #emph[Of allowing servants timely rest.]

Breaks, ease and rest from labor at due times are as needful and necessary as food and clothing. The reason God gives for the fourth commandment shows that masters ought to allow their servants rest. It is this: “so that #footnote[Deut 5:14.] your servant may rest.”

Without breaks and rest the body cannot endure labor: it will grow weak and faint, and utterly unable to continue. But as labor wears down strength, so rest repairs it.

There are two special times of rest which servants must not be denied.

+ The rest of the night.
+ The rest of the Lord’s day.

The first is common to all living creatures; for it was one #footnote[Servants must rest at night.] main reason why God caused the light of the sun to be withdrawn from the face of the earth, that its inhabitants might rest from their labor. “Man #footnote[Ps 104:23.] goes forth to his work and to his labor until evening.” The time of darkness is fitter for rest than for work. Let this not be taken as if no work were to be done after the light of the day is taken away (then only a little work would be done in the depth of winter, when the days are short, and then God would not have provided artificial lights); but it shows that a difference must be made between the day and the night, and that the night is given as the time in which men are chiefly to rest.

The other time of rest (the Sabbath day) is by divine #footnote[Servants must rest on the Lord’s day. Gen 2:3.] institution. It was sanctified for man in the time of Adam’s innocence, when he stood as a public head and stock of all mankind. The very name #emph[Sabbath] (which in Hebrew means #emph[rest]) and the express prohibition of doing any work on that day show that it is a day of rest; and that one end of it was for servants to rest in it is evident from the reason given for it: “so that your servant may rest.” So there is a double bond to tie masters to allow their servants rest on the Lord’s day:

+ The bond of piety to God.
+ The bond of charity to servants: for since God thought it fitting for servants to rest one day in seven, we ought to #footnote[Masters who do not allow servants enough time for sleep offend.] think that it is needful and helpful for them.

=== §. 29. #emph[Of denying servants timely rest.]

Many masters offend against both the times of rest named above: first, those who make their servants stay up too long at night and also rise too early in the morning, not allowing them as much time for rest and sleep as is needful to refresh their bodies and repair their strength. So it comes about that (besides the wrong done to their poor servants) their work cannot be done so well.

#emph[Objection.] It is said of the good housewife named above that “her #footnote[Prov 31:18.] lamp is not extinguished at night.”

#emph[Answer.] That is a #emph[figurative] speech, and somewhat #emph[hyperbolic.] The word #emph[night] is put for a part of it. The phrase only implies watchfulness, showing that she is not, like many, in bed early in the evening and up late in the morning, but late to bed and early up. This phrase (“she rises while it is still night”), #footnote[#emph[v.] 15.] used before, shows that what I have noted is the true sense. If #emph[not extinguishing her lamp at night] should imply sitting up all night long, how could it be said that #emph[she rises?]

#emph[Question.] What time may be thought sufficient to allow for sleep #footnote[How long a time to allow for sleep.] to servants?

#emph[Answer.] As the same quantity of food is not to be measured out too strictly to all alike, so neither is the same length of sleep. Yet experience has shown that for sound and healthy bodies five hours is the least time that may be allowed, and seven hours is time enough for anyone.

=== §. 30. #emph[Of masters’ offense in keeping servants from the rest of the Lord’s day.]

2\. Against the rest of the Lord’s day far too many masters offend, as follows:

1\. By keeping servants at their ordinary work on that day.

2\. By sending them here and there on that day on many errands, such as collecting debts, or doing other services which they are unwilling to allow time for on other days.

3\. By making it the day of greatest pains and labor, particularly for those who are in the kitchen or have any other business about feasts; for many commonly make the Lord’s day a day of feasting.

4\. By keeping them up too late on Saturday nights, even till one o’clock. Many who make some conscience of the Lord’s day are accustomed to offend in this. So that their servants will not work on that day, they keep them up till the very moment they think the Sabbath begins. But in this they commit a double fault: one in not allowing their servants sufficient time for rest; another in making them unfit, through lack of sleep, to do the holy services of the Lord’s day.

=== §. 31. #emph[Of allowing servants time for recreation.]

Concerning times of recreation, I may say as the apostle does in another case, “I have no command from the Lord,” #footnote[1 Cor 7:25.] that is, no express command, to press on the conscience of masters any set time for recreation. Yet without question it is very fitting that servants should have some times to refresh themselves in this way, for recreation rightly used is a great means to put life and add spirit, to youth especially. With work and business not only men’s bodies but their minds also are occupied and employed. In this the labor and toil of men differs from the labor of beasts; and so, for refreshing both body and mind, good and moderate recreation is needful. But I leave this point to the wise consideration of masters themselves, who can discern better by their own observation what is fittest in this respect than we can prescribe.

=== §. 32. #emph[Of masters’ care for their servants in sickness, and after death.]

Masters’ provident care for the good of their servants is not to be limited to the time of their health, but must reach to the time of their sickness also, if it pleases God to visit them with sickness while they are in service. Therefore in this case everything needful is to be provided for them by their master: first, spiritual comfort for their soul, so that they may bear their sickness the better; then such things as may give them ease and refreshment for the present, and also, if it pleases God to add his blessing, bring about a recovery. The centurion’s care for his sick servant#footnote[Matt 8:6.] is commended in Scripture: he did as much for his servant as he could have done for his son. The king of Aram’s care#footnote[2 Kgs 5:6.] was also commendable in this respect: he did not hold back from writing to his enemy and making himself beholden to him on behalf of his servant who was leprous.

If the servant has formerly been a faithful, diligent and #footnote[#emph[Reasons.] 1. Recompense.] profitable servant, recompense requires as much. For it is a most unkind and inhuman thing to deny help in sickness to the servant who in health has been profitable to his master.

But if a master takes care of his sick servant, giving #footnote[2\. Future benefit.] him the best means he can for his ease and recovery, and that servant recovers through God’s blessing on the means, the servant will, if he has any spark of goodness in him, hold himself so obliged to his master that he will never be able to make sufficient recompense, and so he will be moved to use all the diligence he can for his master’s good. So a master may reap benefit for himself by this kind of kindness.

But though the sick servant has in former times #footnote[3\. Charity.] been unprofitable, and there is little hope of future benefit from him, yet for charity’s sake, and in subjection to God’s ordering providence, by which that servant has fallen sick in his master’s house, he must be well cared for in that case. If the Samaritan #footnote[Luke 10:33, 34.] did well in taking care of an utter stranger outside his own house, because he saw him without help, surely the master who neglects his servant who is sick in his house does very ill.

1\. #emph[Question.] What if he is not a servant hired by agreement for a set term, but a journeyman at will, or one who works by the day or the week?

#emph[Answer.] Though a master is not so much bound to such a one, yet if he falls sick in his house, the master must not in that case thrust him out of doors, unless he knows where he may be well provided for.

2\. #emph[Question.] What if a master is poor and not able to provide what is needful for a sick person?

#emph[Answer.] If the sick person has family and relatives who are better able, they must provide for him; if not, the church must help. But it is the master’s duty to make the sick man’s case known to his family or to the church.

If, after all good means are used for his recovery, the servant #footnote[Servants who die are to be decently buried.] dies, his master must see that the last duty is performed for him in some seemly manner, which is Christian burial. In this lies a main difference between the treatment of men’s bodies, which will be raised again after death, and of beasts, which utterly perish. Jacob’s care in burying his mother’s nurse#footnote[Gen 35:8.] is expressly mentioned by way of commendation.

=== §. 33. #emph[Of neglect of servants in sickness, and when they are dead.]

Many masters offend greatly by doing the contrary of the duties named above:

1\. Some, when they see their servants beginning to be sick, will put them out of their houses and leave them to fend for themselves, like that cruel Amalekite who left his sick servant #footnote[1 Sam 30:13.] out in the fields. But note the vengeance of God which followed: leaving that sick servant in that condition was the occasion of the destruction of that master and all his company. By that means they were exposed to their enemies.

2\. Others, when they cannot remove their sick servants from their house, will let them lie there without help and perish for lack of things needful. Many rich men who are well able to provide for them will send them to some out-of-the-way back room and take no more care for them. In this respect many of them show more kindness to a dog, or another beast that is unwell, than to a servant. An inhuman thing!

3\. Others, who seem not so inhuman, deal too harshly with their servants in such a case. They will give them things needful, but all at the poor servants’ cost; and if those servants do not have ready money, they will take it out of their wages if they recover the health to do them service. Is this “to undo the heavy burdens”? Or is it not to lay burden upon #footnote[Isa 58:6.] burden?

4\. Others, who go to some expense over their servants’ sickness, grumble so at their servants and fling out such discontented speeches (namely, that they took them for their work, and not to keep them in their bed; to get something by them, not to be at such cost over them; or that they make themselves sicker than they need to; they could get up if they wanted to; with many other such discontented speeches), that the poor sick servants are more grieved and troubled by their masters’ discontent than by their sickness. And they are often moved to strive beyond their strength to get up, even when death is seizing on them, and so they hasten the approach of death.

5\. Many who may be attentive enough to their servants’ bodily condition in sickness #footnote[See §. 21, 22.] have no regard at all for the spiritual comfort of their souls. They neither give them a word of comfort themselves, nor send for a minister or anyone else to do it, but let them lie in this respect, and die like beasts. Of all kinds of unmercifulness this is the greatest, and the most unbecoming to Christians.

6\. Others, if their servants die, will hardly give them a winding sheet, but say, “Let anyone who will bury them for their clothes.”

These inhuman practices are to be noted, so that the loathing of them may make other masters more tenderhearted toward their servants.

=== §. 34. #emph[Of masters providing for the future livelihood of servants.]

Next to the #emph[soul] and #emph[body] of servants, masters must have some care for their livelihood; and that not only to keep them while they are in their service, but also to strive and provide that they may support themselves and do good to others. “When shall I provide for my own household?”#footnote[Gen 30:30.] says Jacob to his master. This protest shows that this is a master’s duty.

The mind of most masters is contrary to this: in taking on servants they think of nothing but serving their own ends. So it comes about that when masters die many servants are put to very hard straits: some are forced to beg, others are driven to pilfer and steal. In this way caterpillars are bred to plague the commonwealth.

So that I may not be thought to lay the care of parents on masters, and to make servants equal with children, I will point out four particulars which will show what kind of care and provision is required of masters here with respect to their servants’ livelihood.

One is that masters accustom their servants to taking pains.

Another is that they train them in some useful calling.

A third is that they give them sufficient wages.

A fourth is that after sufficient service they allow them to provide for themselves.
